# Lesson 4, batch 2: leading toward two honors. KQ4 in dummy opposite 753 at no-trump; lead toward dummy twice, and both honors score because West has the ace. Keep dummy's ♦A as a way back.
title: Toward the king-queen
id: L04-27
type: exercise
lesson: 4
concepts: 4.4, 3.5
student: S
board: 107
dealer: S
vulnerable: none

deal:            # spades  hearts  diamonds  clubs
  N  KQ4    984    A5     JT763
  E  JT92   32     8762   Q94
  S  753    AK6    KQJ4   A82
  W  A86    QJT75  T93    K5

auction:
  S 1NT    = 15-17 points, balanced
     not *: 17 points and a balanced hand: what does the lesson open?
  W Pass
  N 3NT    = 10+ points: game
  E Pass
  S Pass
  W Pass

auction recap:
  You opened 1NT and partner raised to game. You are declarer, because you
  bid no-trump first, and so West will lead to the first trick.

planning:
  winners: 8
  spades: 1 = The king or queen, once the ace is gone. Both, if West has the ace and you lead toward them twice.
  hearts: 2 = The ace and king.
  diamonds: 4 = Your king, queen and jack, and dummy's ace.
  clubs: 1 = The ace.
  remind: You need 9 tricks. Count the obvious winners in each suit: spades, hearts, diamonds, clubs.
  remind: Where can the ninth trick come from? Which hand should lead that suit, and how often?
play:
  1   W HQ  N H4  E H2  S HA
      note: Eight obvious winners. The ninth can come from spades: lead toward dummy's king-queen twice, and if West has the ace, both honors win a trick.
  2   S S3  W S6  N SK*  E S2
      note: Lead a spade toward dummy.
      SK key: West played low, so play an honor. If West has the ace, it wins.
      SK not S4: Did you think about playing an honor? West has already played, so if West has the ace, the king wins now.
  3   N D5  E D2  S DK  W D3
      note: Back to your hand to lead another spade. Use the ♦K and keep dummy's ace: you'll need it to get back to the ♠Q.
  4   S S5  W SA  N S4*  E S9
      note: Lead toward dummy again.
      S4 key: West took the ace, so keep the queen. It's a winner now.
      S4 not SQ: Did you mean to throw the queen under West's ace? Play low and the queen is a winner.
  5   W HJ  N H8  E H3  S HK
  6   S D4  W D9  N DA  E D6
      note: Over to dummy's ♦A, to cash the ♠Q.
  7   N SQ  E ST  S S7  W S8
      note: That's your ninth trick.
  8   N C3  E C4  S CA  W C5
      note: Back to your hand for the diamonds.
  9   S DQ  W DT  N C6  E D7
  10  S DJ  W H5  N C7  E D8
      note: That's nine tricks.
  11  S H6  W HT  N H9  E SJ
      note: Nothing left to win. Give up the rest.
  12  W H7  N CT  E C9  S C8
  13  W CK  N CJ  E CQ  S C2
      note: 9 tricks: 3NT made.

play recap:
  The plan: eight obvious winners, and a ninth from the spades if West held
  the ace and you led toward dummy's king-queen twice.

  West had to play before dummy each time. When West played low, the king
  won. When West took the ace on the second round, the queen was good. If
  East had held the ace, you'd have made only one spade trick. You also kept
  dummy's ♦A for last, so you could get back to cash the queen.
